<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Saying Please and Thank You – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 10px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Meeting My Friends</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Saying Please and Thank You</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Recognize that people are speaking in English. Interactive: Respond to basic greetings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize their name when called in English and polite words like "please" and "thank you" (e.g., turning their head, making eye contact, or saying "Yes" when their name is called; smiling or pausing when hearing "thank you"). Can respond to greetings and polite words in English, such as "hello," "goodbye," "please," and "thank you" (e.g., saying "hello" when greeted, saying "thank you" when given something, or using "please" when prompted). Can follow basic instructions paired with gestures, such as "say hello" and "wave" (e.g., waving when asked).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a "Hello Song" incorporating simple TPR (waving, clapping). The teacher uses colorful flashcards of "friend" and a puppet to introduce the word, saying "Hello, friend!" while waving the puppet. The teacher then calls out each student's name, pausing for them to respond (e.g., turn their head, make eye contact, or raise a hand), fostering recognition of their own name and the sound of English voices. (Phonemic awareness: environmental sounds of voices saying "hello").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher demonstrates greetings using a puppet named "Happy Henry." Teacher says, "Hello, I am [Teacher's name]!" and waves. Then, with the puppet, "Hello, I am Happy Henry! I am a happy friend." Teacher models "please" by offering a toy to a student with an open palm and saying "Please take the toy." Teacher models "thank you" by receiving the toy back and smiling, saying "Thank you!" The teacher clearly enunciates "friend" with initial /f/ sound and "goodbye" with initial /g/ sound and correct syllable stress.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses gestures and visual cues to check understanding.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher waves) "Is this 'hello'?" (Students nod or shake head).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a flashcard of two children hugging) "Are they 'friends'?" (Students nod or shake head).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher hands an object to a student) "What do I say?" (Teacher prompts for "please" or "thank you" with an expectant look).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Meeting My Friends".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple audio story (e.g., "Molly Meets Her New Friend") with clear, slow English dialogue and relevant sound effects (e.g., children laughing, environmental sounds like gentle rain falling, birds singing). Alternatively, the teacher uses two hand puppets (e.g., Molly and Tom) to act out a short dialogue.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue/Audio Content:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Sound of children playing)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Molly: "Hello! I am Molly. What is your name?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Tom: "Hello, Molly! I am Tom. Nice to meet you."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Molly: "Are you happy to play?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Tom: "Yes, I am happy! Please, can I play with you?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Molly: "Yes! Thank you!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Sound of happy children playing, then "Goodbye!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Can identify common words and simple phrases related to personal information and greetings when spoken slowly and clearly, and follow simple instructions related to classroom routines (Pre-A1.1).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher plays the audio story (or performs the puppet show) for the students.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "Who is talking?" (Teacher points to Molly and Tom flashcards/puppets, students point to the correct one or nod/shake head if the teacher points to the wrong one). "Are they happy?" (Students show thumbs up or smile).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Gestural Response):</b> Teacher makes a statement, students nod for 'true' or shake head for 'false'. "Molly says 'hello'?" (Nod). "Tom says 'goodbye' at the beginning?" (Shake head).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Pointing):</b> Teacher presents flashcards for "hello," "goodbye," "please," "thank you." After hearing each word in the story, students point to the corresponding flashcard.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening to and recognizing names, greetings ("hello," "goodbye"), and polite words ("please," "thank you").
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Subject + Verb ("I am [name]"), WH-questions ("What is your name?"), Yes/No Questions ("Are you Mariana?").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> friend, teacher, name, hello, goodbye, please, thank you, happy, say, wave, meet.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Syllable stress in "friends," "goodbye." Initial /f/ in "friend," /g/ in "goodbye."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Listen and Do!" (TPR/Pointing)</b>
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds up flashcards of "hello," "goodbye," "please," "thank you." The teacher says one word, and students point to the correct flashcard or perform a corresponding action (e.g., wave for "hello," bow for "thank you").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher calls out students' names one by one. Students listen for their name and respond by raising their hand, making eye contact, or turning their head towards the teacher.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says simple instructions with gestures: "Wave hello!" (Students wave). "Wave goodbye!" (Students wave). "Say 'thank you'!" (Students can gesture or try to say "thank you").</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Echo Game" (Choral Repetition)</b>
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says a word or short phrase slowly and clearly, and the students listen and echo it chorally. E.g., Teacher: "Hello!" (Students: "Hello!"). Teacher: "Friend!" (Students: "Friend!"). Teacher: "Thank you!" (Students: "Thank you!").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks Yes/No questions using a puppet: "Are you [student's name]?" (Students nod 'yes' or shake head 'no'). Teacher models the "I am [name]" structure when introducing the puppet.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Interaction: "My Name is..." (Teacher-led questions)</b>
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds "Happy Henry" puppet. Teacher: "Hello, Happy Henry! What is your name?" (Teacher mimics puppet's voice: "My name is Henry!"). The teacher then asks individual students (pointing): "What is your name?" The focus is on students *listening* to the question and recognizing that a response (even a gesture or pointing to self) is expected.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher passes a soft toy to a student saying, "Please take the toy, [student's name]." Students listen and take the toy. Teacher then prompts, "Say 'thank you'!" or models "Thank you!" for students to echo or gesture.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Meeting My Friends".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: "Circle Time Greetings and Passing the Friend"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Teacher-led, highly structured circle time activity with a "friendly" soft toy (e.g., a stuffed animal or "friend" puppet).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students practice listening to greetings and polite words in a simulated "meeting friends" interaction, responding with actions or single words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The class sits in a circle. The teacher starts by holding the "friendly" toy.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "Hello, friends! I am [Teacher's name]." Then, to the student next to them: "Please take the friend, [Student's name]!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The student listens for their name and "please," takes the toy (shows recognition by action).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher then says: "Thank you, [Student's name]!" (Student smiles/nods, showing recognition of "thank you").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The student then passes the toy to the next student, with the teacher prompting: "Say 'Hello' to your friend!" and "Please give the friend to [Next Student's name]!" The focus is on students listening to the teacher's prompts and performing the action.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This continues around the circle. When the toy returns to the teacher, the teacher leads a "Goodbye, friends!" wave.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Throughout the activity, the teacher praises students for listening and responding appropriately.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the "Circle Time Greetings and Passing the Friend" activity and other practice activities, the teacher observes and checks off students' ability to listen and respond non-verbally or with minimal verbal output.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes own name when called (e.g., turns head, makes eye contact, raises hand).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to "hello" (e.g., waves, smiles, makes eye contact).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to "goodbye" (e.g., waves, smiles).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows recognition of "please" (e.g., takes an offered item).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows recognition of "thank you" (e.g., smiles, nods, pauses when hearing the word).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follows basic instructions paired with gestures (e.g., "say hello" by waving, "wave goodbye").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Pre-K Friendly): "Thumbs Up/Down"</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Did you hear 'hello' today?" (Students show thumbs up or down).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Did you hear 'thank you' today?" (Students show thumbs up or down).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection (Teacher guides):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What English words did we hear today?" (Teacher points to flashcards of "hello," "thank you," "friend" and students can point or echo).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which word made you feel happy today?" (Students point to "hello" or "thank you" flashcard, or make a happy face).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to engage Pre-K students in listening for English words and phrases? (e.g., TPR, songs, puppets, specific repetition strategies).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to improve listening comprehension and response for individual students or the class as a whole? (e.g., more visual aids, smaller groups for specific practice, clearer pronunciation modeling, increased use of environmental sounds).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will build on the introduced vocabulary and phrases. Students will recognize the visual forms of these words (e.g., flashcards with words and pictures) and match simple words like "hello," "friend," "thank you" to corresponding pictures, reinforcing the same context of "Meeting My Friends" and "Saying Please and Thank You."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>